    icon: '🃏',
  },
  data: {
    LLM: 'Cards are drawn one at a time anticlockwise; first three players get 22 cards each, last two get 21. During or after drawing, a player may expose an Ace to make it the Big Ace and become declarer; the holder of the matching Ace is the secret partner. Play: declarer or partner (if revealed) leads any single or combination. In turn each player passes or plays a higher combination of the same type. Four consecutive passes clear the pile and the last player leads again. First team to empty both hands wins; scoring is by finish order and doubles if partner revealed their Ace.',
    Player: 'Cards are drawn one at a time anticlockwise; first three players get 22 cards each, last two get 21. During or after drawing, a player may expose an Ace to make it the Big Ace and become declarer; the holder of the matching Ace is the secret partner. Play: declarer or partner (if revealed) leads any single or combination. In turn each player passes or plays a higher combination of the same type. Four consecutive passes clear the pile and the last player leads again. First team to empty both hands wins; scoring is by finish order and doubles if partner revealed their Ace.',
    objective: 'Empty your hand before opponents. The team of the player who goes out first wins; the team of the player who goes out last loses. Score depends on how quickly both team members empty their hands.',
    gameplay: 'Cards are drawn one at a time anticlockwise; first three players get 22 cards each, last two get 21. During or after drawing, a player may expose an Ace to make it the Big Ace and become declarer; the holder of the matching Ace is the secret partner. Play: declarer or partner (if revealed) leads any single or combination. In turn each player passes or plays a higher combination of the same type. Four consecutive passes clear the pile and the last player leads again. First team to empty both hands wins; scoring is by finish order and doubles if partner revealed their Ace.',
    keyRules: [
      'Exposing the matching Ace reveals the partnership but doubles the score.',
      'Four consecutive passes end a round — last player to play leads next.',
      'Bombs beat any non-bomb combination and can be played at any time.',
      'If declarer holds both Aces, they play alone (1 vs 4). They may surrender for a small penalty instead.',
      "Suits don't matter for combinations — only for identifying matching Aces.",
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Exactly 5 players.',
      deck: 'Two standard 54-card decks (2 × 52 + 4 Jokers = 108 cards). Each deck has a red Joker and a black Joker. Suits are irrelevant except for identifying matching Aces.',
      equipment: '108 cards (2 standard decks with Jokers).',
      dealing: 'Cards are drawn one at a time (as in other Chinese games). Players look at cards as they draw, except for the very last card drawn, which is kept face down temporarily. First three players get 22 cards; last two get 21. Play direction is anti-clockwise.',
    },
    turnFlow: 'Cards are drawn one at a time anticlockwise; first three players get 22 cards each, last two get 21. During or after drawing, a player may expose an Ace to make it the Big Ace and become declarer; the holder of the matching Ace is the secret partner. Play: declarer or partner (if revealed) leads any single or combination. In turn each player passes or plays a higher combination of the same type. Four consecutive passes clear the pile and the last player leads again. First team to empty both hands wins; scoring is by finish order and doubles if partner revealed their Ace.',
